\section{Conventions common to all components}{

All geometries are two-dimensional \code{sf} geometries in British National Grid, EPSG:27700 (OSGB36 / British National Grid), in
metres. They were georeferenced and spatially transformed after the original dataset was distributed, so they are not the original,
unmodified coordinates; the original local drawing coordinates are not included. Axial-line identifiers are the original identifiers: they are
unique but are not row numbers and need not be consecutive. Every \code{axial_id} in \code{unlinks} and \code{gates} refers to a
parent axial line in the \code{axial} object, not to a segment generated later from the network. Unavailable
observations are \code{NA}, never zero. Text categories are lower-case snake case. The list returned by
\code{\link[=load_example_data]{load_example_data()}} carries the full description of origin and conventions in its \code{metadata} attribute.
}

\section{\code{axial}}{

An \code{sf} data frame with one \code{LINESTRING} per axial line.
\describe{
\item{\code{axial_id}}{integer, the original axial-line identifier.}
\item{\code{source_connectivity}}{integer, the supplied historical connectivity of the line.}
\item{\code{source_control}}{numeric, the supplied historical control value.}
\item{\code{source_integration}}{numeric, the supplied historical integration value.}
\item{\code{source_local_integration}}{numeric, the supplied historical local integration value.}
\item{\code{geometry}}{\code{LINESTRING}, EPSG:27700.}
}
The four \code{source_} columns are values supplied with the original data, unchanged and not recalculated. They are not rPSTool or PST results,
